{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 613 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "34e27d668a1f45b761bbc3d76824b46c4d36442eb838c836cc31258310d04ad1" :edb/db-before-t 612 :edb/db-after-t 613 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533925 50 #inst "2026-10-05T13:28:44.504Z" 13194139533925 true] [17592186096613 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266169 13194139533925 true] [17592186096613 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266176 13194139533925 true] [17592186096613 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266239 13194139533925 true] [17592186096613 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266246 13194139533925 true] [17592186096613 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266260 13194139533925 true] [17592186096614 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266183 13194139533925 true] [17592186096614 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266190 13194139533925 true] [17592186096614 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266253 13194139533925 true] [17592186096614 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266267 13194139533925 true] [17592186096615 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266197 13194139533925 true] [17592186096615 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266204 13194139533925 true] [17592186096615 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266211 13194139533925 true] [17592186096615 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266281 13194139533925 true] [17592186096616 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266218 13194139533925 true] [17592186096616 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266225 13194139533925 true] [17592186096616 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266232 13194139533925 true] [17592186096616 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266274 13194139533925 true] [17592186096922 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533925 true] [17592186096922 1145 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(8,12),$ so we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=12$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {8}^{2} & =4p(12) \\\\ 64 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{4}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533925 true] [17592186096928 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533925 true] [17592186096928 1145 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-4,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}=4p(y-4).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(0,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-4-4) \\\\ 16 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})(y-4) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-2(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533925 true] [17592186096930 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533925 true] [17592186096930 1145 "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(3,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y-0) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4py.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-4,7),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=7$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-4-3)^{2} & =4p(7) \\\\ 49 & =28p \\\\ p & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{7}{4})y \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =7y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533925 true] [17592186266169 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266170 13194139533925 true] [17592186266169 1089 "q-115415" 13194139533925 true] [17592186266169 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533925 true] [17592186266169 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/52b283fd5a8d9f374fad0a007f127598b86c53d7acf4df05d1415589e43faaa0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533925 true] [17592186266169 1113 #uuid "9d68b304-5abf-5a45-9d18-e5b77fc90d3e" 13194139533925 true] [17592186266169 1145 "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(3,1).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(3,1),$ so we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {3}^{2} & =4p(1) \\\\ 9 & =4p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533925 true] [17592186266170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266171 13194139533925 true] [17592186266170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266172 13194139533925 true] [17592186266170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266173 13194139533925 true] [17592186266170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266174 13194139533925 true] [17592186266170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266175 13194139533925 true] [17592186266170 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533925 true] [17592186266170 1041 "selection" 13194139533925 true] [17592186266170 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266174 13194139533925 true] [17592186266170 1110 #uuid "ba3a9320-0a9e-5b7a-8e31-95cffb38d468" 13194139533925 true] [17592186266171 1034 "{x}^{2}=-9y" 13194139533925 true] [17592186266171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533925 true] [17592186266171 1111 #uui